      "quote": "The findings of this review have differential implications depending on healthcare system architecture. In Beveridgean systems (e.g., UK NHS), direct access has been implemented through First Contact Practitioner (FCP) models embedded within general practice, with physiotherapists serving as first point of contact for MSK complaints. The evidence synthesized here supports the continued expansion of such models, with demonstrated reductions in GP workload and comparable clinical outcomes. In Bismarckian systems such as France and Germany, where physiotherapy traditionally requires medical prescription and reimbursement is tied to physician referral, implementation faces additional regulatory and cultural barriers. In France, despite recent regulatory developments -including the 2023 Rist law introducing limited direct access [20], the 2024 implementing decree expanding its scope within organised primary care settings [21], and the ministerial order of 25 June 2025 further clarifying eligible conditions and professional requirements [52]-, DA remains limited by: (i) legal requirements for medical prescription; (ii) fee-for-service reimbursement structures not adapted to first-contact physiotherapy; (iii) historical professional boundaries; and (iv) variable physiotherapist training in differential diagnosis and redflag screening. Our findings suggest that, if these barriers were addressed, direct access could contribute to alleviating the well-documented pressures on French general practice, particularly in underserved areas. In liberal healthcare systems (e.g., USA), where DA legislation varies by state, the evidence supports unrestricted access models, with studies consistently showing lower costs and comparable outcomes. However, implementation considerations differ regarding insurance coverage and out-of-pocket costs for patients. Fig. 3 Methodological quality distribution (ROB 2.0 / NOS) Across all systems, successful implementation requires: clear triage protocols and red-flag screening competencies; explicit thresholds for medical referral; robust communication channels between physiotherapists and physicians; context-specific reimbursement models; and ongoing monitoring of workload, outcomes, and safety.",
